Reach std::out_ptr through vte::out_ptr, for a libstdc++ without it

This tree asks for gnu++23 and uses std::out_ptr (P1132R7) at three call
sites.  The feature landed in libstdc++ 14; the compiler this port is built
with is GCC 13.3, whose front end accepts gnu++23 and whose library has every
other C++23 feature VTE uses but not this one.  The build stops with

  'out_ptr' is not a member of 'std'; did you mean 'auto_ptr'?

Rather than rewrite the three call sites into raw-pointer-then-reset -- which
is what std::out_ptr exists to avoid writing -- the name is indirected through
vte::out_ptr in std-glue.hh, beside take_freeable and the other adapters of
that kind.  Where __cpp_lib_out_ptr is defined it IS std::out_ptr and nothing
changes; where it is not, a fourteen-line adapter provides the only behaviour
these call sites use: convert to T**, and adopt what was stored when the full
expression ends.

--- a/src/std-glue.hh
+++ b/src/std-glue.hh
@@ -31,6 +31,45 @@
 template<typename T>
 inline auto take_freeable(T* t) { return Freeable<T>{t}; }
 
+/* std::out_ptr, for a standard library that does not have it yet.
+ *
+ * P1132R7 landed in C++23 and in libstdc++ 14.  This tree asks for gnu++23
+ * and is built here with libstdc++ 13, which has every other C++23 feature it
+ * uses and not this one -- so the three call sites are spelled vte::out_ptr
+ * and resolve to the standard one wherever it exists.
+ *
+ * The semantics are the whole of what those call sites need: hand a T** to a C
+ * function that allocates, and adopt whatever it stored when the full
+ * expression ends.  A temporary of this type is destroyed after the call it
+ * was an argument to has returned, which is what makes the reset() land at the
+ * right moment -- the same mechanism the standard version uses.
+ */
+#if defined(__cpp_lib_out_ptr)
+template<typename SmartPtr>
+inline auto out_ptr(SmartPtr& p) { return std::out_ptr(p); }
+#else
+template<typename SmartPtr>
+class OutPtr {
+public:
+        using pointer = typename SmartPtr::pointer;
+
+        explicit OutPtr(SmartPtr& p) noexcept : m_owner{p} { }
+        ~OutPtr() { m_owner.reset(m_raw); }
+
+        OutPtr(OutPtr const&) = delete;
+        OutPtr& operator=(OutPtr const&) = delete;
+
+        operator pointer*() noexcept { return &m_raw; }
+
+private:
+        SmartPtr& m_owner;
+        pointer m_raw{nullptr};
+};
+
+template<typename SmartPtr>
+inline auto out_ptr(SmartPtr& p) { return OutPtr<SmartPtr>{p}; }
+#endif
+
 #define VTE_DECLARE_FREEABLE(T, func) \
 template<> \
 class FreeableDeleter<T> { \
